  "text": "עצמאי\n### Step 1: Understand the Problem\nWe need to find the locus of the centers \\((a,b)\\) of all circles that intersect the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\) at two distinct points, such that the line segment connecting these points is perpendicular to the tangent line to the parabola at its midpoint.\n\n### Step 2: Equation of the Tangent to the Parabola\nFirst, find the equation of the tangent to the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\) at a general point \\((x, y)\\). The slope of the tangent at \\((x, y)\\) is given by the derivative:\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2}{y}\n\\]\nSo, the equation of the tangent line at \\((x, y)\\) is:\n\\[\ny - y = \\frac{2}{y}(x - x)\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{2}{y}x\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nxy = y^2\n\\]\nSince we are at a specific point on the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\), the tangent line equation becomes:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{x}{\\frac{4}{2}}y = \\frac{x}{2}y\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{x}{2}y\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{x}{2}y\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{x}{2}y\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{d}{2}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Midpoint of the Tangent Line\nThe midpoint of the line segment connecting the two points of intersection will be the same as the point where the tangent touches the parabola, which is \\((\\frac{y^2}{4}, y)\\).\n\n### Step 4: Condition for Perpendicularity\nThe line segment connecting the two points of intersection is perpendicular to the tangent line at its midpoint. The slope of the line segment is given by the coordinates of the intersection points. We need to find the condition for perpendicularity.\n\n### Step 5: Circle Equation\nThe circle equation is:\n\\[\n(x - a)^2 + (y - b)^2 = r^2\n\\]\nWe need to find the radius \\(r\\). Since the line segment is perpendicular to the tangent at the midpoint, we can use the geometry of the situation to find the condition on \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\n### Step 6: Locus of the Centers\nAfter solving the conditions, we find that the locus of the centers \\((a,b)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n(x_1 + x_3)(x_2 + x_4) > 2(x_1 - x_2)(b_1 - b_2)\n\\]\nBut since \\(x_2 = 3a - 4b + 2a^2\\), we get:\n\\[\n(x_1 + 3a - 4b + 2a^2)(x_2 + 4b + 2a^2) > 2(x_1 - x_2)(b_1 - b_2)\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n(x_1 + 3a - 4b + 2a^2)(x_2 + 4b + 2a^2) > 2(a^2 - 3a + 2b - 2a^2 - 4b + 8a - 6b)\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_1 + 3a - 4b + 2a^2)(x_2 + 4b + 2a^2) > -4a + 4b\n\\]\nAfter further simplification, we get:\n\\[\n2a^2 + b^2 - a - b = 0\n\\]\n\n### Step 7: Final Answer\nTherefore, the locus of the centers is given by:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2a^2 + b^2 - a - b = 0}\n\\]",
